\description{
A mixing layer draws contacts afresh every day, by group, instead of
keeping ties. Its contacts are given in one of two ways:
\itemize{
\item A contact matrix, for a few groups that meet each other (e.g., age groups
in public spaces): entry \verb{(g, h)} is the expected number of contacts per day
of an agent in group \code{g} with agents in group \code{h}.
\item A contact probability per pair, for many small groups that mix only within
(households, classrooms): each member meets each other member of its group
with probability \code{contact_prob} per day, whatever the size of the group.
\code{contact_prob = 1} makes every group a clique (everyone meets everyone,
every day), with no ties stored.
}
}
\details{
\code{layer_mixing()} creates one to add to any model with \code{\link[=add_layer]{add_layer()}}. Every
agent of a group takes part in the mixing (the contacts are spread over all
of them), and the agents in the model's infectious states transmit (see
\code{\link[=set_infectious_states]{set_infectious_states()}}; e.g., Infected, not Exposed, in \code{\link[=ModelSEIR]{ModelSEIR()}}).

With \code{set_contact_rate()}, the contacts of a layer with a contact matrix are
a model parameter times the matrix. The parameter is read at the end of
every day, so a global event or a calibration that changes it changes the
next day's contacts. With everyone in one group and a 1 x 1 matrix of 1,
every agent meets "Contact rate" others a day, at random (what the former
connected models, e.g. \code{ModelSIRCONN()}, did; see the example).

Give exactly one of \code{contact_matrix} and \code{contact_prob}. The number of
groups is the number of rows of \code{contact_matrix}, or the length of
\code{contact_prob}; with a single \code{contact_prob}, it is \code{max(groups) + 1}.

As for every layer, an edit made while the model runs (e.g., from a global
event) lasts for that run only, and one made between runs is kept. A move
made with \code{set_group()} during a day takes effect at the end of the day.
A layer with a contact matrix and one with contact probabilities cannot be
swapped during a run.
